Interest rates and loan terms. The annual percentage rates (APRs) and terms that lenders may offer you will depend on factors such as your credit history, the length of the loan, and the vehicle's age and value.1 Lenders will offer their most competitive rates if you can show a history of on-time payments, particularly on previous auto loans.
Loan amount and repayment options. The loan amount will depend on how large a down payment you are prepared to make, how much you need to borrow, and how much the lender is willing to lend you based on your creditworthiness. You may have the option to spread your payments over anywhere from 36 to 72 months, or even longer. But bear in mind that while a longer-term loan may mean lower payments each month, it will cost you more in the long run due to the interest charges.
Fees and additional costs. Private party auto loans may have origination or application fees, as well as prepayment penalties if you want to pay off the loan earlier than expected. Many loans also impose late fees if you're tardy with a payment.
